COMMIT 232ed9dde0113d4b79fd6fb2186e21b3847db6fd
fix(core): give a swallowed interrupt back to the caller of closeAll

Producers.closeAll turns whatever a producer's close() throws into a
failed future, so the loop finishes and no later producer is stranded
(FIX-141). When that throw is a sneaky InterruptedException, swallowing
it left the caller's next blocking call unaware that its thread had been
cancelled. Three lines restore the flag, which is D-098 (1).

The decision here is the PLACEMENT, not the guard. It sits in the
synchronous catch rather than in the recover lambda, because that lambda
also serves producers whose future fails later: there the failure is
observed on whichever thread completed it, and setting the flag would
invent an interrupt nobody raised, which D-098 (2) refuses.

Three cases, each killing a different mutant. Only the third separates
the two placements, because a recover body on an already-failed future
runs inline on the caller's thread, so a misplaced guard still sets the
flag on the very thread doing the asserting. That case is green on
arrival and earns its place by the mutant it kills, logged with its diff
before the run and cmp after the restore.

Review FAILed twice and both were mine. First: the mutation kills were
claims with no log on disk, and the placement was pinned by nothing.
Second: the comment said D-056 widened this catch. It did not. D-056
widened Delivery.java:176 and :218; FIX-141 and F-30 widened this one,
which the comment one line above already said. I commissioned that
citation and accepted it without checking, with the fact sitting in a
packet I had written myself. D-056 is now cited nowhere in the file --
the reviewer's reason for dropping it rather than demoting it is that
keeping it would attribute a rule to a decision that does not contain
it, which is the F-27 defect class.

Evidence: scoped PIT on Producers has 0 survivors and 0 without
coverage. Honestly that is 6 killed by named tests and 1 TIMED_OUT
unattributed on untouched line 67, not the 7 killed PIT rolls it up as;
that line is now FIX-154. Module verify -Pit green, core 566 with 0
skipped, coverage no decrease, and the FIX-144 flake did not fire.
Counts reconcile exactly: 563 after FIX-142 plus three cases is 566, and
the class goes 19 to 22.

Residual: FIX-153, where the restore belongs, as a decision rather than
a refactor -- the same sneaky throw can mean the caller interrupted this
thread while it blocked in close(), and nothing in the code can tell
that from a producer's own interrupt.

